---
const worksTotal = 6739;
const sectorCounts = [2985, 1246, 1435, 426, 344];
const sectorRest = worksTotal - sectorCounts.reduce((sum, value) => sum + value, 0);

const sectors = [
  { name: 'Film', count: 2985, tone: 'one' },
  { name: 'Portfolio cases', count: 1246, tone: 'two' },
  { name: 'Music tracks and albums', count: 1435, tone: 'three' },
  { name: 'Animation', count: 426, tone: 'four' },
  { name: 'Theatre', count: 344, tone: 'five' },
  { name: 'Digital art and games', count: sectorRest, tone: 'six', remainder: true },
].map((row) => ({
  ...row,
  pct: (row.count / worksTotal) * 100,
}));

const milestoneCells = [
  ...Array.from({ length: 58 }, () => 'antigravity'),
  ...Array.from({ length: 34 }, () => 'opencode'),
  ...Array.from({ length: 4 }, () => 'codex'),
];

const recoveredYears = 529;
const boxOfficeNaive = 16.4;
const boxOfficeDeduped = 10.48;
const boxOfficePct = (boxOfficeDeduped / boxOfficeNaive) * 100;

const audienceMax = 17.4;
const audienceTheatrical = 11.0;
const audienceMusic = 17.4;
---
<div class="dossier">
  <nav class="case-nav" aria-label="Case sections">
    <a href="#brief">The brief</a>
    <a href="#scale">130M+ rows</a>
    <a href="#scraping">Web scraping</a>
    <a href="#filtering">Entity resolution</a>
    <a href="#market-model">Market model</a>
    <a href="#agents">Agent pipeline</a>
    <a href="#delivery">Delivery</a>
    <a href="#stack">Stack</a>
  </nav>

  <section class="dsec" id="brief" aria-labelledby="brief-title">
    <div class="mark" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="mark-n">1,886</span>
      <span class="mark-note">participation rows on input. 186 carried a profile link.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="sbody">
      <h2 id="brief-title">Eight days to measure seven seasons of an industry.</h2>
      <p class="standfirst">
        A national talent competition needed to measure the industry footprint of its finalists
        across seven seasons. The team had started collecting credits by hand and asked whether automated
        scrapers could extract the rest: films, series, music releases, theatre
        productions, games, and festival projects since 2020, with audience reach, box office revenue,
        and ratings.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="ledger">
      <ol class="ledger-rows" role="list">
        <li class="ledger-row">
          <span class="ledger-n">1,886</span>
          <span class="ledger-t">
            participation rows on a flat spreadsheet, across 1,513 professionals in film, theatre,
            music, digital, and events. Only 186 carried a single profile link.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="ledger-row">
          <span class="ledger-n">130M+</span>
          <span class="ledger-t">
            records screened from bulk dumps, state registries, streaming catalogs, and live
            scrapers, mirrored into local SQLite FTS5 indexes.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="ledger-row">
          <span class="ledger-n">6,739</span>
          <span class="ledger-t">
            credited works resolved, with 2,735 verified professional profiles behind them.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <p class="ledger-foot">
        Eight days. 585 Python scripts. 20 source modules. 70 canonical sources. One encrypted deck.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="sbody">
      <p>
        On input I received a flat spreadsheet of 1,886 participation rows covering 1,513 unique
        professionals across five creative sectors: film, theatre, music, digital, and events. The
        rest of each row held a name, a birth date, a city, a discipline, and a season.
      </p>
      <p>
        Searching 1,513 people one by one through live web queries would have hit rate limits on the
        first afternoon and drowned in namesake noise. I built a data-engineering pipeline to resolve
        the roster at scale: eight logged days of ingestion and screening that ended with 6,739
        credited works and 2,735 verified profiles.
      </p>
      <p class="note">
        Client identity, participant names, and project titles are withheld by agreement.
        The metrics, source classes, and throughput figures below come from production logs.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="dsec" id="scale" aria-labelledby="scale-title">
    <div class="mark" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="mark-n">130M+</span>
      <span class="mark-note">records screened against 1,513 names, through 70 canonical sources.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="sbody">
      <h2 id="scale-title">I pulled external catalogs onto local disk before matching a single name.</h2>
      <p>
        Live APIs cap requests at 200 to 500 calls a day, making cross-referencing 1,513 people across four
        name variants impractical over the network. To bypass quota walls, I downloaded bulk dumps and open
        registries, converted them into local SQLite databases with FTS5 indexes, and streamed
        multi-gigabyte archives in constant memory. Local queries run in under 1 millisecond and consume zero API quota.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="fig fig-flat">
      <h3 class="fig-head">IMDb held four rows in five.</h3>
      <p class="cap">
        Rows screened per bulk source, full ingest. Crimson marks the IMDb pass. One tick = 1M
        rows, rounded to the nearest million; registries and Steam fall below the unit and stand
        as short stubs with exact labels. The axis is honest: no break, no log scale, IMDb simply
        towers.
      </p>
      <svg
        id="lf-sources"
        viewBox="0 0 760 344"
        role="img"
        aria-label="Tick rows of rows screened per bulk source: IMDb 102.6 million, Discogs and MusicBrainz 10.28 million, Yandex Music 6.04 million, TMDB 5.05 million, Kinopoisk 1.06 million, state registries 380 thousand, Steam 148,909."
      ></svg>
      <figcaption class="cap">
        All seven sources share one linear scale, so the IMDb dump alone is almost the whole
        picture. It was filtered in a single 150 s streaming pass across 15.8M names and 12.9M
        titles.
      </figcaption>
      <table class="sr-only">
        <caption>Rows screened per bulk source, with run detail where logged</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr><th scope="col">Source</th><th scope="col">Rows</th><th scope="col">Run</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">IMDb official TSV datasets</th><td>102.6M</td><td>150 s streaming pass</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Discogs and MusicBrainz</th><td>10,284,281</td><td>288 s via iterparse</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Yandex Music</th><td>6,039,445</td><td>Sub-millisecond B-tree lookups</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">TMDB</th><td>5,051,606</td><td>56,598 rows/s in constant memory</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Kinopoisk</th><td>1,060,836</td><td>38 MB constant RAM</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">State registries (MKRF, UNF, FPG, PFKI)</th><td>380,000+</td><td>Certificates, plays, grants</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Steam Games dataset</th><td>148,909</td><td>16 s full ingest</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </figure>

    <div class="sbody sbody-tail">
      <div class="pair">
        <div class="pair-cell">
          <p class="pair-n">22.4M</p>
          <p>
            artist and creator records indexed locally across Discogs, Yandex Music, TMDB, and
            Kinopoisk dumps.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="pair-cell">
          <p class="pair-n">56,598/s</p>
          <p>rows screened per second against the person index with a flat 38 MB RAM ceiling.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="dsec" id="scraping" aria-labelledby="scraping-title">
    <div class="mark" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="mark-n">32</span>
      <span class="mark-note">platforms scraped. Five of them needed a different way through.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="sbody">
      <h2 id="scraping-title">Browser relays and direct protocol calls handled the live web.</h2>
      <p>
        Bulk dumps cover encyclopedias and state registries. Creative professionals publish across
        dozens of live platforms: Vimeo showreels, Behance and Dprofile portfolios, ArtStation,
        VKontakte, Telegram channels, rights societies, and legacy cinema portals. Each one blocks
        automated collection differently: strict daily quotas, Cloudflare Turnstile, DDOS-GUARD image
        captchas, JA3/JA4 TLS fingerprinting, or 401 walls on unauthenticated endpoints.
      </p>
      <p>
        Five target layers carried the load. Open a layer to read what the wall was and how it fell.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="fig fig-flat">
      <div class="evidence-list">
        <details class="evidence">
          <summary>
            <span class="ev-name">Vimeo and Next.js state JWTs</span>
            <span class="ev-yield">
              353 video works across 30 verified channels and 43 finalists, plus full crew lists
              that exposed cross-disciplinary collaborations.
            </span>
          </summary>
          <div class="ev-body">
            <p>
              Extracted the public client JWT embedded inside the Next.js state of any profile page,
              then queried the internal catalog API at 0.2 s per channel.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="evidence">
          <summary>
            <span class="ev-name">Dprofile and Кино-Театр.РУ behind auth and captchas</span>
            <span class="ev-yield">
              650+ design cases, unmasked contacts, and legacy filmographies that returned HTTP 401
              or image captchas to headless browsers.
            </span>
          </summary>
          <div class="ev-body">
            <p>
              Attached the Playwright MCP Extension to a live authenticated desktop Chrome session
              and ran batched in-page fetch loops inside the origin.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="evidence">
          <summary>
            <span class="ev-name">Rights society (RAO) and federal portals</span>
            <span class="ev-yield">
              314 registered musical scores, stage designs, and scripts across 1,532 scanned names,
              plus 37 subsidized cinema PDFs.
            </span>
          </summary>
          <div class="ev-body">
            <p>
              The WAF dropped Python HTTP clients on TLS fingerprinting. Requests went through the
              OS native Schannel stack with curl at a 100% pass rate, and PDFs parsed at 340 pages/s.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="evidence">
          <summary>
            <span class="ev-name">Kinopoisk APIs and EAIS box office</span>
            <span class="ev-yield">
              Box office revenue, ticketed attendance, and viewer ratings across commercial
              theatrical releases and streaming originals.
            </span>
          </summary>
          <div class="ev-body">
            <p>
              Paired a two-tier SQLite disk cache (MAX_SAFE_LIVE_CALLS = 175) with direct JSON
              lookups by state certificate number at 0.05 s per film.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="evidence">
          <summary>
            <span class="ev-name">VKontakte, Telegram, Exa, Apify and OSINT</span>
            <span class="ev-yield">
              3,880 profiles across 32 platforms, 660 works from a 7,060-message Telegram archive,
              and 240 Tilda results pages parsed by coordinates.
            </span>
          </summary>
          <div class="ev-body">
            <p>
              Server-side VKScript execute batches carried 25 profiles per call, backed by a
              35,000-member community index, Exa semantic search, Maigret, and GHunt.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="cap">
        Network routing split by geography. International platforms ran through a transparent VLESS
        Reality TUN adapter (happ-xray) or Apify cloud actors, while domestic registries ran direct
        to avoid geo-blocks.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="sbody sbody-tail">
      <p>
        The highest-yield trick was the Cross-Credits Graph on Vimeo and YouTube. Video descriptions
        list full crews: director, cinematographer, editor, colorist,
        sound designer, and VFX artist. Parsing crew blocks from 30 verified channels and
        matching names against the 1,513-person index verified works for several finalists across
        disciplines from one scrape.
      </p>
      <p>
        When a legacy cinema portal blocked the IP after 100 requests and held only 0.7% of 698
        scanned finalists, I stopped broad collection. I used the live browser relay to finish the
        5 pending hits.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="dsec" id="filtering" aria-labelledby="filtering-title">
    <div class="mark" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="mark-n">97%</span>
      <span class="mark-note">of raw name matches were noise before the gates.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="sbody">
      <h2 id="filtering-title">Matching 1,513 people against 130 million rows is a filtering problem.</h2>
      <p>
        Thousands of people share the same first and last name. Among the participants, 441 were
        juniors aged 14 to 17 with minimal public records, unlisted birth years, or stage names.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="fig fig-flat">
      <h3 class="fig-head">97% of raw name matches belonged to somebody else.</h3>
      <div class="noise">
        <div class="noise-row">
          <div class="noise-head">
            <span class="noise-src">IMDb name matches</span>
            <span class="noise-val">2.8% real</span>
          </div>
          <div class="noise-track" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="noise-hit" style="width:2.8%"></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="noise-row">
          <div class="noise-head">
            <span class="noise-src">MobyGames name matches</span>
            <span class="noise-val">4.3% real</span>
          </div>
          <div class="noise-track" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="noise-hit" style="width:4.3%"></span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="noise-key" aria-hidden="true">
        <span class="noise-dot"></span> crimson is the actual finalist. Ink is everyone with the same name.
      </p>
      <figcaption class="cap">
        Raw name matching produced 97% noise across the corpus: only 2.8% of IMDb matches and 4.3%
        of MobyGames matches belonged to the actual finalist.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="sbody sbody-tail">
      <p>Every enricher ran a multi-factor filter stack before a candidate record could touch the database.</p>
      <div class="gates">
        <div class="gate">
          <h3>Bilingual Cyrillic and Latin, plus stage names</h3>
          <p>
            Every person was queried across four representations: Cyrillic forms, bidirectional
            Latin transliteration (-iy/-y, kh/h, zh/j), social handles, and verified stage names.
            Adding Latin search uncovered 400+ releases for media composers who publish exclusively
            in English.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="gate">
          <h3>Token-set intersection, never substring matching</h3>
          <p>
            Substring checks matched a German specialist named <em>Anna Simon</em> inside the Russian
            surname <em>Simonova</em>. The matcher requires exact token-set intersection with
            normalized Slavic gender endings (-ov/-ova, -in/-ina, -sky/-skaya).
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="gate">
          <h3>Anti-Senior chronological filter</h3>
          <p>
            The dominant error is not a random stranger. It is an older industry veteran with the
            same name and decades of credits. Any candidate with a work released before the
            finalist’s birth year plus 13, or plus 16 for adults, is disqualified in full.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="gate">
          <h3>Patronymic checks and institutional exclusions</h3>
          <p>
            Where a registry or track credit carried a conflicting patronymic, the match was dropped
            immediately. Context regexes also stripped institutional false positives such as film
            schools or foundations named after a famous surname.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="cap">
        Retrieval and verification stayed separate gates. Even when a hard target came out of an
        authenticated human browser session, three of five retrieved candidates were still rejected
        on a birth-date mismatch.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="outcomes">
      <div class="kept">
        <h3>6,739 works survived the filter.</h3>
        <div class="stack-bar" aria-hidden="true">
          {
            sectors.map((sector) => (
              <span class={`seg seg-${sector.tone}`} style={`width:${sector.pct.toFixed(3)}%`}></span>
            ))
          }
        </div>
        <ul class="stack-legend" role="list">
          {
            sectors.map((sector) => (
              <li class={`legend-row legend-${sector.tone}`}>
                <span class="legend-sw" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <span class="legend-name">{sector.name}</span>
                <span class="legend-count">{sector.count.toLocaleString('en-US')}</span>
              </li>
            ))
          }
        </ul>
        <p class="cap">
          6,739 active credited works. Digital art and games account for the remaining 303.
        </p>

        <div class="waffles">
          <div class="waffle-cell">
            <h3>95 of every 100 finalists were enriched.</h3>
            <svg
              id="lf-enriched"
              viewBox="0 0 400 320"
              role="img"
              aria-label="Tick gauge showing 95.0 percent of the 1,513-person roster enriched, 76 people without a verified record."
            ></svg>
            <p class="waffle-t">
              Roster coverage after eight days: 1,437 of 1,513 professionals. Crimson ticks mark
              the enriched; one tick = 1% of the roster. The 76 people without a verified record
              stay on file as not found rather than guessed.
            </p>
            <table class="sr-only">
              <caption>Roster enrichment coverage</caption>
              <thead>
                <tr><th scope="col">State</th><th scope="col">People</th><th scope="col">Share</th></tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                <tr><th scope="row">Enriched with verified profiles or works</th><td>1,437</td><td>95.0%</td></tr>
                <tr><th scope="row">Without a verified record</th><td>76</td><td>5.0%</td></tr>
              </tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <div class="waffle-cell">
            <div class="waffle" aria-hidden="true">
              {Array.from({ length: 100 }, (_, i) => <span class={i < 92 ? 'on' : 'off'}></span>)}
            </div>
            <p class="waffle-n">91.8%</p>
            <p class="waffle-t">6,186 works carry a direct source URL. 1,034 people have confirmed projects on file.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="rejected">
        <h3>1.87M+ rows were rejected.</h3>
        <ul class="reject-list" role="list">
          <li>
            <span class="reject-n">1,304,911</span>
            <span>senior namesake records, plus 569,429 unrelated cast and crew rows, rejected in the Kinopoisk dump pass alone.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="reject-n">648</span>
            <span>false namesakes rejected on TMDB, and 160 works from 3 senior namesakes blocked at the rights registry.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <span class="reject-n">76</span>
            <span>people left honestly as not found rather than padded with unverified guesses.</span>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="dsec" id="market-model" aria-labelledby="market-model-title">
    <div class="mark" aria-hidden="true">
      <span class="mark-n">56%</span>
      <span class="mark-note">overstatement from summing box office per person.</span>
    </div>

    <div class="sbody">
      <h2 id="market-model-title">Translating scraped rows into market metrics.</h2>
      <p>
        The client needed headline numbers for a seven-year impact report across five creative
        sectors: total projects, active specialists, audience reach, and financial volume. Turning
        6,739 scraped works into numbers that survive executive scrutiny meant solving three
        analytical traps: double-counting collaborative blockbusters, mixing project budgets with
        personal income, and missing release years.
      </p>
    </div>

    <figure class="fig fig-flat">
      <h3 class="fig-head">One blockbuster was counted once per finalist.</h3>
      <div class="bo">
        <div class="bo-row">
          <div class="bo-head">
            <span>Summed per finalist</span>
            <span class="bo-val">16.4B</span>
          </div>
          <div class="bo-track" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="bo-block bo-naive" style="width:100%"></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="bo-row">
          <div class="bo-head">
            <span>Grouped by work, MAX</span>
            <span class="bo-val">10.48B</span>
          </div>
          <div class="bo-track" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="bo-block bo-true" style={`width:${boxOfficePct.toFixed(2)}%`}></span>
            <span class="bo-gap">56% inflation removed</span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="cap">
        RUB. Two finalists on the same feature film produced two revenue rows. Summing them
        overstated box office by 56%, 16.4B against 10.48B. The rule: group by work, take MAX,
        never SUM.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="fig fig-flat">
      <h3 class="fig-head">Market output and personal income are not the same money.</h3>
      <div class="layers">
        <div class="layer-row">
          <div class="layer-head">
            <span>Gross market output</span>
            <span class="bo-val">14B+</span>
          </div>
          <div class="layer-out" aria-hidden="true">
            <span ></span>
          </div>
          <p class="layer-note">Box office, stage budgets, grants, game capitalization.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="layer-row">
          <div class="layer-head">
            <span>Direct specialist income and royalties</span>
            <span class="bo-val">1.2–1.8B</span>
          </div>
          <div class="layer-in-wrap" aria-hidden="true">
            <span class="layer-in"></span>
          </div>
          <p class="layer-note">Fees and royalties. Separated from film gross.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="cap">
        The two layers that never get summed: a composer’s royalty and a feature film’s box office
        are measured separately and reported separately.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <figure class="fig fig-flat">
      <h3 class="fig-head">Reach came in three contours that do not add up cleanly.</h3>
      <div class="reach">
        <div class="reach-row">
          <div class="reach-head">
            <span>Deduplicated theatrical admissions</span>
            <span class="bo-val">11.0M</span>
          </div>
          <div class="reach-track" aria-hidden="true">
            <span style={`width:${((audienceTheatrical / audienceMax) * 100).toFixed(2)}%`}></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="reach-row">
          <div class="reach-head">
            <span>Six-year music streaming plus soundtrack cinema reach</span>
            <span class="bo-val">17.4M</span>
          </div>
          <div class="reach-track" aria-hidden="true">
            <span style="width:100%"></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="reach-row reach-row--text">
          <div class="reach-head">
            <span>Steam players, domestic and global</span>
            <span class="bo-val">held apart</span>
          </div>
          <p class="layer-note">Player counts stay separate from the audience total.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <figcaption class="cap">
        Two audience contours on one scale, plus a third that is reported separately. 36.8M viewer
        votes across 1,956 rated works sit behind them.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <div class="sbody sbody-tail">
      <p>
        Because the client asked specifically for works released since 2020, an undated row was a
        direct loss of reporting coverage. At the start, 1,340 active works, 26.0% of the corpus, had
        no release year. I built a seven-stage cascade ordered from free local indexes to
        quota-capped APIs.
      </p>

      <div class="cascade-wrap">
        <p class="cascade-axis mono">free local index <span aria-hidden="true">→</span> quota-capped API</p>
        <ol class="cascade" role="list">
          <li>Local ministry registry title matching</li>
          <li>Title year regexes</li>
          <li>Vimeo v2 upload timestamps</li>
          <li>Yandex Music track credits</li>
          <li>PoiskKino searches</li>
          <li>Kinopoisk Unofficial keyword lookups</li>
          <li>Studio date cards</li>
        </ol>
        <div class="cascade-out">
          <p class="cascade-n">529</p>
          <p class="cascade-t">
            release years recovered. Undated works fell from 26.0% to 15.8% of the corpus, then the
            cascade stopped where the data ends.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p class="cap">
          165 works live in a rights registry with no date column at all, and 22 one-word short films
          cannot be disambiguated from older films with the same title.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="boundary">
        <p>
          <strong>Null is unknown, never zero.</strong> When a film had fewer votes than a platform’s
          publication threshold, 138 titles, or no public box office, the database stores NULL rather
          than 0. Averages and medians stay honest: 7.20 mean and 7.4 median across film and
          animation.
        </p>
      </div>

      <p class="note">
        Every macro metric is backed by a SQL view and a formula printed on the slide. The 2020–2026
